<header><p class="st-kicker">other · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Territory of Hawaii</h1><p class="st-meta">1898 CE – 1959 CE · capital Honolulu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Hawaii's six decades as an American territory, dominated by sugar companies and transformed by Pearl Harbor, before it became the 50th state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> annexed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-hawaii">Republic of Hawaii</a> in 1898, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaiian-organic-act">Organic Act</a> of 1900 organised the islands as a territory, granting US citizenship to its citizens and an elected legislature, with a governor appointed by the president. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanford-b-dole">Sanford B. Dole</a>, president of the republic, became the first territorial governor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Five corporations, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/big-five-hawaii">Big Five</a>, controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, shipping and banking, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-dole">James Dole</a> made the islands the world's leading pineapple producer. Japanese immigrants and their children became the largest ethnic group. The Japanese attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a> on 7 December 1941 brought martial law until 1944. Nisei soldiers of the 442nd Regimental Combat Team won fame in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and in 1954 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-united-states">Democrats</a> backed by labour unions ended Republican dominance.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Statehood was long delayed by racial prejudice and Southern opposition. Congress passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii-admission-act">Admission Act</a> in 1959, voters approved it overwhelmingly, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> became the 50th state on 21 August 1959.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/territory-of-hawaii.jpg" alt="Territory of Hawaii" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Dbenbenn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hawaii.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1898 CE – 1959 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Honolulu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sanford B. Dole</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Hawaiian, Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hawaii Territory</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1928&amp;focus=territory-of-hawaii" aria-label="Territory of Hawaii on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="439 1390 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Territory of Hawaii lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M759 1646L759 1646M759 1646L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M759 1646h0M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M759 1646h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M759 1646h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Honolulu. <a href="/atlas?year=1928&amp;focus=territory-of-hawaii">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
